pairs** were identified.\n\n| Feature A | Feature B | Correlation |\n|------------|------------|------------:|\n| Ch3_Std | Ch3_Q3 | 0.9773 |\n| Ch3_Q1 | Ch3_Q3 | 0.9539 |\n| Ch4_Std | Ch4_Q3 | 0.9664 |\n| Ch4_Q1 | Ch4_Q3 | 0.9526 |\n| Ch5_Std | Ch5_Q1 | 0.9636 |\n| Ch5_Q1 | Ch5_Q3 | 0.9565 |\n| Ch7_Std | Ch7_Q1 | 0.9702 |\n| Ch7_Std | Ch7_Q3 | 0.9584 |\n| Ch7_Q1 | Ch7_Q3 | 0.9659 |\n\nThese highly correlated features mainly involve **standard deviation (Std)** and **quartile-based features (Q1 and Q3)** extracted from the same EMG channel. Since these statistics describe similar aspects of signal dispersion, strong correlations between them are expected.\n\n---","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"# ============================================================\n# Section F.3 : Correlation Heatmap\n# ============================================================\n\nimport matplotlib.pyplot as plt\n\nplt.figure(figsize=(18, 14))\n\nimg = plt.imshow(corr_matrix, cmap=\"coolwarm\", vmin=-1, vmax=1)\n\nplt.colorbar(img, label=\"Correlation Coefficient\")\n\nplt.title(\"Correlation Heatmap of Statistical Features\")\n\nplt.xticks(\n    range(len(corr_matrix.columns)),\n    corr_matrix.columns,\n    rotation=90,\n    fontsize=6\n)\n\nplt.yticks(\n    range(len(corr_matrix.columns)),\n    corr_matrix.columns,\n    fontsize=6\n)\n\nplt.tight_layout()\n\nplt.show()","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-07-25T21:46:52.768950Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-07-25T21:46:52.769388Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-07-25T21:46:53.935850Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-07-25T21:46:52.769361Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-07-25T21:46:53.934647Z"}},"outputs":[{"output_type":"display_data","data":{"text/plain":"<Figure size 1800x1400 with 2 Axes>","image/png":"\n"},"metadata":{}}],"execution_count":122},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"## Effect on Machine Learning\n\nOnly a few features are highly correlated, so the dataset has **low feature redundancy**.\n\nHighly correlated features can:\n\n- Increase training time.\n- Provide similar information.\n- Make some models harder to interpret.\n\nTree-based models such as **Random Forest**, **XGBoost**, **CatBoost**, and **LightGBM** can handle correlated features well. If needed, techniques like **feature selection** or **PCA** can be used to remove redundant features and reduce the number of features.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"# Section G: Low-Dimensional Visualization Using PCA, t-SNE, and UMAP\n\nThe extracted statistical feature set consists of **72 numerical features** for each of the **3420 EMG recordings**. Since visualizing data in a 72-dimensional space is not feasible, dimensionality reduction techniques were applied to project the data into a two-dimensional space.\n\nThree commonly used visualization techniques were employed:\n\n- **Principal Component Analysis (PCA)**\n- **t-distributed Stochastic Neighbor Embedding (t-SNE)**\n- **Uniform Manifold Approximation and Projection (UMAP)**\n\nThese techniques help evaluate whether the extracted statistical features naturally separate the twelve gesture classes.\n\n---","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"## G.1 Principal Component Analysis (PCA)\n\nPrincipal Component Analysis (PCA) is a linear dimensionality reduction technique that projects high-dimensional data onto a smaller number of orthogonal components while preserving as much variance as possible.\n\nThe two principal components were used to visualize the statistical feature space.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"# ============================================================\n# Section G.1 : Prepare Data\n# ============================================================\n\nfrom sklearn.preprocessing import StandardScaler\n\n# Standardize the statistical features\nscaler = StandardScaler()\n\nX_scaled = scaler.fit_transform(stats_df)\n\n# Labels\ny = metadata_df[\"Gesture\"]\n\nprint(\"Feature Matrix Shape:\", X_scaled.shape)","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-07-25T21:46:53.940000Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-07-25T21:46:53.940380Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-07-25T21:46:53.957505Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-07-25T21:46:53.940349Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-07-25T21:46:53.956368Z"}},"outputs":[{"name":"stdout","text":"Feature Matrix Shape: (3420, 72)\n","output_type":"stream"}],"execution_count":123},{"cell_type":"code","source":"\n#  PCA (2D)\n\n\nfrom sklearn.decomposition import PCA\nimport matplotlib.pyplot as plt\n\npca = PCA(n_components=2)\n\nX_pca = pca.fit_transform(X_scaled)\n\nplt.figure(figsize=(10,8))\n\ngestures = sorted(y.unique())\n\nfor gesture in gestures:\n    mask = (y == gesture)\n    plt.scatter(\n        X_pca[mask,0],\n        X_pca[mask,1],\n        s=20,\n        alpha=0.7,\n        label=gesture\n    )\n\nplt.xlabel(\"Principal Component 1\")\nplt.ylabel(\"Principal Component 2\")\n\nplt.title(\"PCA Projection of FORS-EMG Features\")\n\nplt.legend(bbox_to_anchor=(1.05,1), loc=\"upper left\")\n\nplt.tight_layout()\n\nplt.show()\n\nprint(\"Explained Variance Ratio:\")\nprint(pca.explained_variance_ratio_)","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-07-25T21:46:53.958948Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-07-25T21:46:53.959343Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-07-25T21:46:54.516863Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-07-25T21:46:53.959296Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-07-25T21:46:54.515432Z"}},"outputs":[{"output_type":"display_data","data":{"text/plain":"<Figure size 1000x800 with 1 Axes>","image/png":"\n"},"metadata":{}},{"name":"stdout","text":"Explained Variance Ratio:\n[0.18744339 0.11379817]\n","output_type":"stream"}],"execution_count":124},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"## Result\n\nThe feature matrix contains **3420 samples** and **72 statistical features**.\n\nPCA reduced the data from **72 dimensions to 2 dimensions** for visualization.\n\nThe first two principal components explain:\n\n- **PC1:** 18.74% of the total variance\n- **PC2:** 11.38% of the total variance\n\nTogether, the first two principal components explain **31.12%** of the total variance in the dataset. This indicates that PCA captures a meaningful portion of the data, although some information is lost when reducing the data to two dimensions.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"## G.2 t-Distributed Stochastic Neighbor Embedding (t-SNE)\n\nt-SNE is a nonlinear dimensionality reduction technique designed to preserve local neighborhood relationships between samples.\n\nCompared with PCA, t-SNE generally produces more compact clusters and provides better visualization of complex datasets.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"# ============================================================\n# Section G.2 : t-SNE (2D)\n# ============================================================\n\nfrom sklearn.manifold import TSNE\n\ntsne = TSNE(\n    n_components=2,\n    random_state=42,\n    perplexity=30\n)\n\nX_tsne = tsne.fit_transform(X_scaled)\n\nplt.figure(figsize=(10,8))\n\nfor gesture in gestures:\n\n    mask = (y == gesture)\n\n    plt.scatter(\n        X_tsne[mask,0],\n        X_tsne[mask,1],\n        s=20,\n        alpha=0.7,\n        label=gesture\n    )\n\nplt.title(\"t-SNE Projection of FORS-EMG Features\")\n\nplt.xlabel(\"t-SNE Component 1\")\nplt.ylabel(\"t-SNE Component 2\")\n\nplt.legend(\n    bbox_to_anchor=(1.05,1),\n    loc=\"upper left\"\n)\n\nplt.tight_layout()\n\nplt.show()","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-07-25T21:46:54.518235Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-07-25T21:46:54.518756Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-07-25T21:47:14.960822Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-07-25T21:46:54.518722Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-07-25T21:47:14.959489Z"}},"outputs":[{"output_type":"display_data","data":{"text/plain":"<Figure size 1000x800 with 1 Axes>","image/png":"\n"},"metadata":{}}],"execution_count":125},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"### Observation\n\nThe **t-SNE** visualization shows better separation between gesture classes than **PCA**. Several gesture classes form distinct clusters, although some similar hand gestures still overlap.\n\nThis indicates that the extracted statistical features capture useful nonlinear patterns that can help models to classify different gestures.\n","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"## G.3 Uniform Manifold Approximation and Projection (UMAP)\n\nUMAP is another nonlinear dimensionality reduction technique that preserves both local and global data structure while providing computationally efficient visualizations.\n\n","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"# ============================================================\n# Section G.4 : UMAP (2D)\n# ============================================================\n\n# Install UMAP if needed (Kaggle usually has it preinstalled)\n# !pip install -q umap-learn\n\nimport umap\nimport matplotlib.pyplot as plt\n\n# Create UMAP model\nreducer = umap.UMAP(\n    n_neighbors=15,\n    min_dist=0.1,\n    metric=\"euclidean\",\n    n_components=2,\n    random_state=42\n)\n\n# Fit and transform\nX_umap = reducer.fit_transform(X_scaled)\n\nprint(\"UMAP Output Shape:\", X_umap.shape)\n\n# Plot\nplt.figure(figsize=(10, 8))\n\ngestures = sorted(y.unique())\n\nfor gesture in gestures:\n    mask = (y == gesture)\n\n    plt.scatter(\n        X_umap[mask, 0],\n        X_umap[mask, 1],\n        s=18,\n        alpha=0.75,\n        label=gesture\n    )\n\nplt.title(\"UMAP Projection of FORS-EMG Statistical Features\")\nplt.xlabel(\"UMAP Component 1\")\nplt.ylabel(\"UMAP Component 2\")\n\nplt.legend(\n    bbox_to_anchor=(1.02, 1),\n    loc=\"upper left\",\n    fontsize=8\n)\n\nplt.grid(alpha=0.3)\nplt.tight_layout()\n\nplt.show()","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-07-25T21:47:14.962265Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-07-25T21:47:14.962738Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-07-25T21:47:33.298626Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-07-25T21:47:14.962689Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-07-25T21:47:33.297357Z"}},"outputs":[{"name":"stderr","text":"/usr/local/lib/python3.12/dist-packages/umap/umap_.py:1952: UserWarning:\n\nn_jobs value 1 overridden to 1 by setting random_state. Use no seed for parallelism.\n\n","output_type":"stream"},{"name":"stdout","text":"UMAP Output Shape: (3420, 2)\n","output_type":"stream"},{"output_type":"display_data","data":{"text/plain":"<Figure size 1000x800 with 1 Axes>","image/png":"\n"},"metadata":{}}],"execution_count":126},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"### Observation\n\nThe UMAP projection shows compact clusters with better separation between many gesture classes. Compared with PCA, UMAP provides a clearer view of the structure in the EMG features. However, some similar gestures still overlap because they have similar muscle activation patterns. This shows that completely separating all gesture classes in just two dimensions is difficult.\n","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"## G.4 Comparison of PCA, t-SNE, and UMAP\n\n| Method | Characteristics | Class Separation |\n|---------|-----------------|------------------|\n| PCA | Linear projection preserving global variance | Partial separation with noticeable overlap |\n| t-SNE | Nonlinear projection preserving local neighborhoods | Better cluster formation and improved separation |\n| UMAP | Nonlinear projection preserving local and global structure | Compact clusters with clearer separation than PCA |\n\n---\n\n## G.5 Do the Classes Separate?\n\n### Dimensionality Reduction Summary\n\nThe dimensionality reduction results show that the extracted statistical features contain useful information for recognizing different hand gestures.\n\n* **PCA** shows the overall structure of the dataset but has significant overlap between gesture classes.\n* **t-SNE** creates more compact clusters and provides better separation between gestures.\n* **UMAP** also shows clear clusters while preserving the overall structure of the data.\n\nAlthough the gesture classes are not completely separated in two dimensions, the visualizations indicate that the extracted features contain enough useful information for machine learning models to distinguish between different hand gestures.\n\n\n---","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"# Section H: Interactive Data Visualization\n\nInteractive visualizations were developed using **Plotly** to provide an intuitive exploration of the FORS-EMG dataset. Unlike static plots, interactive figures allow zooming, panning, and hover-based inspection of individual observations.\n\nThe following interactive visualizations were created:\n\n- Interactive Class Distribution Bar Chart\n- Interactive Box Plot\n- Interactive Violin Plot\n- Interactive PCA Scatter Plot\n\nThese visualizations complement the previous exploratory analysis by enabling detailed inspection of feature distributions and class separability.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"### H.1 Reading\n\nThe interactive bar chart confirms that each of the twelve gesture classes contains **285 recordings**. The equal heights of the bars verify that the dataset is perfectly balanced, reducing the risk of bias during model training.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"# ============================================================\n# Section H.1 : Interactive Class Distribution\n# ============================================================\n  \nimport plotly.express as px\n\nfig = px.bar(\n    class_counts,\n    x=\"Gesture\",\n    y=\"Count\",\n    title=\"Interactive Class Distribution\",\n    text=\"Count\",\n    color=\"Count\"\n)\n\nfig.update_layout(\n    xaxis_title=\"Gesture\",\n    yaxis_title=\"Number of Recordings\",\n    template=\"plotly_white\"\n)\n\nfig.show()","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-07-25T21:47:33.300004Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-07-25T21:47: